From 14a81cabbc53f1462e5f41812766218184083c2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: logdog2325 <loganjayfisher@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 11:08:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Battle Style: pick your trainer model, the music, and the
 venue

Three cosmetic selectors for VS-mode battles, all riding the machinery that
already ships "$XD OU Fixes": the host assembles one Action Replay block from
the choices and SyncCodes delivers it to everyone, so both clients always run
identical codes and nothing new crosses the wire.

Each player picks their own trainer model. The guest's choice travels in the
team submission as a "Model:" header beside the existing "Name:" one; the
host's "Guest model" dropdown is only the fallback when a guest never picks --
the same precedence the slot-3 team fallback uses. Music and venue belong to
the host, like the battle rules screen does.

"Game default" everywhere means the code is ABSENT, not a write of the vanilla
value: an untouched launcher produces a byte-identical session to the previous
build. The block lives in the local GXXE01.ini for exactly the session's
duration, and the writer only touches the file when it actually has something
to add or remove, because IniFile's round trip normalizes hand-kept comments
away and this path runs on every room close for every game.

When cosmetics are picked with the OU toggle off, cheats are forced on for the
session (the AR engine is gated on them) while a local disable keeps OU Fixes
itself off; that force is re-decided at every start, not latched, so a block
that empties between battles in the same room gives the flag straight back --
otherwise game two would silently run OU rules the host's toggle refuses.

The addresses: trainer models patch the li-r3 sites of Ralf's NTSC-U model
modifiers (ids 0x01..0x43); music pins the three-slot staBGM table at
0x802EAF0C, verified against a clean main.dol (u32 stride, vanilla 0x4F3 =
stm_bgm_tool_minnade); venues write the battlefield index at +0x06 of the four
GBA-link battle records in the resident common.rel image, with the indirection
proven by disassembly of the record accessors. Entries whose terrain changes
Nature Power, Camouflage or Secret Power say so in their labels.
